From: rhodep port <none@example.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 01:55:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panel: nt37701: restore mode_flags on error, and add
 bl_lpm

Two things, and the honest framing matters because one of them does not
do what it was written for.

The real fix is the error path. nt37701_bl_update_status() cleared
MIPI_DSI_MODE_LPM, sent the brightness, and returned early if the write
failed -- leaving the host in HS mode for every command that followed,
which the panel does not recover from short of a power cycle. mode_flags
is now saved and restored unconditionally.

The second is bl_lpm, a writable parameter that sends the brightness in
LP escape mode instead. It was an attempt at the glitched horizontal
lines this panel paints while the brightness ramps down, on the theory
that a HS write can be inserted into the DPU's frame stream at a packet
boundary -- mid-frame from the panel's point of view -- while LP forces
the host to leave the HS burst first. msm's cmd_mutex does not help
either way: it serialises DCS transfers against each other, not against
the DPU's frame pushes, which never go through dsi_host_transfer().

It does not work. Tested against the real workload, KWin's ambient
brightness with a torch on the sensor, the lines are identical with
bl_lpm=1 and bl_lpm=0.

What the attempt did produce is the clue for whoever picks this up.
Synthesising the same write pattern through sysfs does not reproduce the
artefact at all -- not at 2 writes/s with 3200-step jumps, and not at
KWin's own measured 14 writes/s for seconds at a time. The difference is
that those writes land against a static screen, and on a command mode
panel a static screen means there are no frame transfers to collide
with. So the trigger needs brightness writes concurrent with the
compositor repainting, which also explains the asymmetry the user
reported: KWin ramps up in ~13 writes over 0.8 s and down in 60-180
writes over 3.5-7.2 s, and only the long downward ramp shows it.

dsi_err_worker: status=5 does fire during the real workload, but it does
not track either the mode or the write rate under synthetic load (0
errors at 14/s, 3 at 4/s), so it is not usable as a proxy yet.

The parameter is kept because it costs nothing, the panel demonstrably
accepts brightness in LP -- actual_brightness reads back correctly -- and
it is the first thing the next person will want to rule out. The default
is LP on theoretical grounds only.

Signed-off-by: rhodep port <none@example.com>
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-novatek-nt37701.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-novatek-nt37701.c
@@ -61,6 +61,36 @@
 module_param(pps_first, bool, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(pps_first, "enviar PPS antes de la secuencia de encendido (default 0)");
 
+/*
+ * Send the brightness DCS write in LP escape mode instead of HS.
+ *
+ * This was an attempt at the glitched horizontal lines this panel paints while
+ * the brightness is ramping down, on the theory that a HS write can be inserted
+ * into the DPU's frame stream at a packet boundary -- mid-frame as far as the
+ * panel is concerned -- whereas in LP the host must first take the lanes out of
+ * the HS burst, so the write lands between frames by construction. msm's
+ * cmd_mutex does not help either way: it serialises DCS transfers against each
+ * other, not against the DPU's frame pushes, which do not go through
+ * dsi_host_transfer() at all.
+ *
+ * IT DOES NOT FIX THE ARTEFACT. Tested on the device against the real workload
+ * -- KWin's ambient brightness with a torch on the sensor -- and the lines are
+ * identical with bl_lpm=1 and bl_lpm=0. Nor does synthesising the same write
+ * rate through sysfs reproduce them at all, which is the useful clue: those
+ * writes happen against a static screen, and in command mode a static screen
+ * means no frame transfers to collide with. Whatever this is, it needs the
+ * compositor repainting at the same time.
+ *
+ * The option is kept because it costs nothing, the panel demonstrably accepts
+ * brightness in LP (actual_brightness reads back correctly), and it is the
+ * obvious thing for the next person to want to rule in or out. The default is
+ * LP on theoretical grounds only; bl_lpm=0 restores the original behaviour at
+ * runtime and is equally correct as far as anything measured here shows.
+ */
+static bool bl_lpm = true;
+module_param(bl_lpm, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(bl_lpm, "send brightness in LP mode, avoiding mid-frame writes (default 1)");
+
 static const struct regulator_bulk_data nt37701_supplies[] = {
 	{ .supply = "vddio" },
 	{ .supply = "vci" },
@@ -359,15 +389,23 @@
 	u16 brightness = backlight_get_brightness(bl);
 	int ret;
 
-	dsi->mode_flags &= ~MIPI_DSI_MODE_LPM;
+	unsigned long saved = dsi->mode_flags;
+
+	if (bl_lpm)
+		dsi->mode_flags |= MIPI_DSI_MODE_LPM;
+	else
+		dsi->mode_flags &= ~MIPI_DSI_MODE_LPM;
 
 	ret = mipi_dsi_dcs_set_display_brightness_large(dsi, brightness);
-	if (ret < 0)
-		return ret;
 
-	dsi->mode_flags |= MIPI_DSI_MODE_LPM;
+	/*
+	 * Restore unconditionally. The old code returned early on error and left
+	 * the host in HS for every command that followed, which the panel never
+	 * recovers from short of a power cycle.
+	 */
+	dsi->mode_flags = saved;
 
-	return 0;
+	return ret < 0 ? ret : 0;
 }
 
 // TODO: Check if /sys/class/backlight/.../actual_brightness actually returns
